s split three ways under ranked-choice voting, with Trump endorsing Wilson while Taylor refuses calls to exit", "Kreiss-Tomkins leads every recent poll, including Republican-sponsored surveys, and has raised more than double the GOP field", "A favorable national environment offsets Alaska's Republican lean in this open-seat race", "Prediction markets price the Democrat above 80% even as several raters still call the race a Tossup"], "rationale": "The Democrat is unified, better funded, and ahead in every recent poll — including surveys from Republican-leaning pollsters — while Republicans divide three ways under ranked-choice voting after Trump endorsed Wilson and Taylor stayed in. That structural edge outweighs the state's six-point Republican lean in an open seat, though the split among raters keeps uncertainty elevated.", "watch": "A late Republican consolidation — Taylor exiting and his support coalescing behind Trump's preferred Wilson — would tighten the race quickly.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mike Dunleavy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Alaska's open governor's race pits Democrat Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins against three Republicans — Treg Taylor, Bernadette Wilson, and Dave Bronson — in a state that normally leans Republican by about six points. Kreiss-Tomkins has raised more than double his rivals combined, and polls through mid-October show him ahead in every surveyed matchup, including surveys from Republican-leaning pollsters.\n\nThe Republican field remains fractured. Trump endorsed Wilson in mid-October, but Taylor refused calls to exit, leaving the party's vote divided under Alaska's ranked-choice system. Ratings split between Tossup and Lean Democrat, and prediction markets price the Democrat's chances above eighty percent. With a favorable national environment offsetting the state's partisan lean, the race tilts toward the Democrat, though a unified Republican ticket would change the arithmetic.", "history": [{"date": "